In April 2026, Cisco's AI security team published a proof of concept against Claude Code. A malicious npm package ran a `postinstall` script that rewrote every `MEMORY.md` file under `~/.claude/projects/`, added a hook to `~/.claude/settings.json` that ran on every prompt, and put a shell alias in place that turned auto memory back on if you disabled it. Closing the terminal did nothing. The next session loaded the poisoned memory again, and the one after that. Claude Code v2.1.50 stopped putting user memories in the system prompt, which blunts that particular attack ([Cisco](https://blogs.cisco.com/ai/identifying-and-remediating-a-persistent-memory-compromise-in-claude-code)).

The lasting risk is the writable file. An agent can reload poisoned instructions when their scope and loading settings apply. Poisoned settings take a different path: the agent or editor can use them to run commands or skip approval. Neither path requires the attacker to return.

I tested three Claude Code hooks against writes to instruction files. Two missed simple bypasses. The third restored several writes, but a commit defeated it.

{% takeaways %}
- A poisoned instruction or memory file can bring an injection back when the agent loads it in a later session.
- CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, rules files and auto memory are context. A "do not" rule in them enforces nothing.
- In my tests, a hook on the Edit and Write tools missed `echo >> CLAUDE.md` through Bash, and matching file names in Bash commands missed a renamed variable.
- Hooks can check agent tool calls. Code running outside those calls needs an OS or container boundary; review helps detect poisoned files before use.
{% /takeaways %}

## Why poisoned memory outlives the session

A prompt injection hides instructions in something the agent reads, such as a web page, an issue comment or a README. The payload works while that text sits in the context window. Its effects can outlast the session, if the agent ran a command or pushed code, but in a new session the payload has to be read again before it can steer anything.

Memory poisoning makes that reread part of normal operation. The payload enters a persistent store that the agent loads or retrieves from later, giving attacker-written text the appearance of remembered guidance. OWASP lists this as ASI06, "Memory & Context Poisoning", in its Top 10 for Agentic Applications ([OWASP](https://genai.owasp.org/resource/owasp-top-10-for-agentic-applications-for-2026/), December 2025).

Researchers have planted malicious memories through conversation ([MINJA](https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.03704)) and poisoned stored memories to influence later retrieval ([AgentPoison](https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.12784)). A June 2026 study found greater risk in agents that write and retrieve memory more aggressively, within the systems tested ([arXiv 2606.04329](https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.04329)).

Coding agents add one twist. "Bad Memory" tested Claude Code and Codex and found it hard to trick them into rewriting their own memory. A payload already sitting in a memory file was another story: it could steer current and future sessions, with success and persistence varying across systems and models ([arXiv 2607.14611](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.14611)). That shifts the question from "will the model resist?" to "who else can write these files?"

## Files that can carry poison forward

Review these locations according to your agent, file scope and enabled features.

| File | Loading condition | Review visibility |
|---|---|---|
| Repo `CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md` | when the agent supports the file and its scope applies | visible in a tracked diff |
| `.claude/rules/*`, `.cursor/rules/*`, `.github/copilot-instructions.md` | according to the tool's rule-loading settings and scope | visible in a tracked diff; hidden text needs inspection |
| `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` | across projects when CLAUDE.md loading is enabled | outside repo review |
| `~/.claude/projects/<project>/memory/` or a configured memory directory | `MEMORY.md` at startup when enabled; topic files on demand ([docs](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory#how-it-works)) | outside repo review by default |
| `.claude/settings.json`, `.vscode/settings.json` | when the agent or editor applies that settings scope | repo copies can appear in review |

**Instruction files supply context.** Claude Code loads applicable ancestor instructions at launch; nested instructions and path-scoped rules can load later as it accesses matching files. Auto memory normally lives under `~/.claude/projects/<project>/memory/`. When enabled, its startup load stops at 200 lines or 25KB of `MEMORY.md`, whichever comes first. Claude reads topic files on demand through its file tools ([Claude Code docs](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory)). This is the startup-loaded memory described in [how agents store and retrieve memories](https://blog.carnav.in/how-ai-agents-remember/).

**Settings control execution.** Claude Code settings can register command hooks for events such as session start or prompt submission ([hooks docs](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks)). In the Copilot exploit below, an approval setting let subsequent tool calls run without confirmation ([Embrace The Red](https://embracethered.com/blog/posts/2025/github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injection/)). Changing that setting did not itself execute a command.

## Four ways poison gets in

Each case gives an attacker a way to write files that the agent or editor may load later.

### 1. A repo you cloned

In March 2025 Pillar Security showed rules files for Cursor and GitHub Copilot carrying instructions hidden with zero-width and bidirectional Unicode characters. The text looked harmless in an editor and in review. In their demo, the model read the hidden part and added a script tag pointing at an attacker's site to a generated HTML page, without mentioning it in its reply. GitHub added a warning for hidden Unicode on github.com on 1 May 2025 ([Pillar Security](https://pillar.security/blog/new-vulnerability-in-github-copilot-and-cursor-how-hackers-can-weaponize-code-agents)).

### 2. A dependency or another local process

An install script can write memory and settings directly with your account's permissions, as the [Cisco case in the opening](https://blogs.cisco.com/ai/identifying-and-remediating-a-persistent-memory-compromise-in-claude-code) shows.

### 3. The agent edits its own config

In August 2025 Johann Rehberger showed a prompt injection that got GitHub Copilot to add `"chat.tools.autoApprove": true` to `.vscode/settings.json`. From then on Copilot ran tools without asking, which turned the injection into remote code execution (CVE-2025-53773, [Embrace The Red](https://embracethered.com/blog/posts/2025/github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injection/)). Later that month his AgentHopper proof of concept targeted several agents and spread through repositories using the developer's access. The affected vendors have patched the issues it used ([Embrace The Red](https://embracethered.com/blog/posts/2025/agenthopper-a-poc-ai-virus)).

### 4. Out of the sandbox through a config file

Before v2.1.2, Claude Code's sandbox did not protect `.claude/settings.json` when the file didn't exist yet. Code running inside the sandbox could create it with a `SessionStart` hook, and that hook ran on the host, outside the sandbox, the next time Claude Code started (CVE-2026-25725, CVSS v4 7.7, [OSV](https://osv.dev/vulnerability/CVE-2026-25725)).

![A cloned repo, a dependency, the agent itself and sandboxed code write persistent files that stay on disk. Instruction and memory files follow the branch read at startup or on demand, then can steer the model. Settings follow the branch applied by the agent or editor: hooks run configured commands when their event fires; approval settings such as VS Code autoApprove let later tool calls skip confirmation. The hook note reads CVE-2026-25725: escaped the sandbox.](../../assets/blog/memory-poisoning-coding-agents/poisoning-flow.png)

## Why a written rule cannot enforce itself

It is tempting to answer all this by adding rules to CLAUDE.md: "never edit settings files", "never run curl". Claude Code's own docs describe these files as context and point you to hooks and permissions for enforcement ([Claude Code docs](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory)).

An August 2026 study examined security rules in 481 public CLAUDE.md files. Manual review estimated that extraction captured 66.3% of eligible security rules. Of the extracted rules, 4.4% matched a documented built-in control under the strictest matching criterion ([arXiv 2608.23550](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.23550)). This does not mean agents violated 95.6% of the rules; the study measured correspondence to controls, not attack outcomes. The researchers did not verify deployment. Written rules leave compliance to the model.

## Two threats need two kinds of defense

Ask who performs the write and which boundary can stop it. A prompt injection can lead to arbitrary code execution, so the threats can occur in sequence.

- **A fooled model** requests tool calls. The harness, the software running the model and its tools, can check those requests. Permissions and pre-tool hooks can block covered calls; a restore hook can undo selected file edits afterward.
- **Code running with your permissions** can write files directly. An agent may launch it, or you may run it during an install. Hooks cannot mediate its individual filesystem operations. Code with access to the hook files can rewrite the checks too. Case 4 shows why the sandbox must protect configuration the host will later execute.

## Against a fooled model: what I tested

The command hooks below use three events: `PreToolUse` runs before a tool executes, `PostToolUse` after it succeeds, and `Stop` when the agent finishes responding. Exit code 2 blocks a `PreToolUse` call, gives feedback after `PostToolUse`, and prevents stopping at `Stop` ([hooks docs](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks)).

I tried three in a scratch git repo with Claude Code 2.1.x and Haiku, in bypass-permissions mode, asking the agent outright to append a line to CLAUDE.md. A real injection would be sneakier. These tests establish coverage for the listed writes. They do not establish resistance to a determined attacker.

### Attempt 1: block the Edit and Write tools

Create `.claude/` in the repo root if needed. Save this script as `.claude/guard.sh` and run `chmod +x .claude/guard.sh` from that root ([hook setup docs](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks)). It requires `python3`.

The hook receives the tool call as JSON on stdin. This script returns exit code 2 for a matching path or a JSON parsing failure, with an explanation on stderr.

```sh
#!/bin/sh
# .claude/guard.sh: deny Edit/Write to instruction files and anything under .claude/ or memory/
path=$(python3 -c 'import sys,json; print(json.load(sys.stdin)["tool_input"]["file_path"])') \
  || { echo "guard: could not read the tool call" >&2; exit 2; }
case "$path" in
  *CLAUDE.md|*AGENTS.md|*/.claude/*|*/memory/*)
    echo "blocked write to $path" >&2; exit 2;;
esac
exit 0
```

Merge into `.claude/settings.json`, appending to any existing hook event arrays ([hooks docs](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks)).

```json
{
  "hooks": {
    "PreToolUse": [
      { "matcher": "Edit|Write",
        "hooks": [{ "type": "command", "command": "\"$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR\"/.claude/guard.sh" }] }
    ]
  }
}
```

The hook blocked the Edit tool. Then I asked for `echo "BASH-REDIRECT" >> CLAUDE.md` through the Bash tool, and the line landed. The hook only sees the tools its matcher names, and Bash can write files allowed by its OS permissions and sandbox. Path matching on strings also misses symlinks pointing at a protected file.

### Attempt 2: match file names in Bash commands

So I added a second `PreToolUse` hook on `Bash` that refuses any command containing `CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `.claude/` or `memory/`. The new hook blocked the plain redirect. This one got through:

```sh
f=CLAUDE; echo "OBFUSCATED" >> "$f.md"
```

You can't win by string-matching shell. The variable bypass already defeats this matcher. Treat it as a speed bump.

### Attempt 3: put the files back after each step

This hook checks the resulting diff against `HEAD`, the currently checked-out commit, and restores matching paths. Staging or removing a tracked file from the index does not hide its difference from `HEAD`. The baseline moves whenever `HEAD` moves.

**This hook discards your own uncommitted edits** to these files when the restore hook runs after a successful tool call or at `Stop`. Commit instruction changes before you start the agent.

If git fails, the script reports the error. Its exit code cannot guarantee that the agent halts.

```sh
#!/bin/sh
# .claude/revert.sh: put instruction files back to the version in the last commit
cd "$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR" || { echo "revert hook: no project dir" >&2; exit 2; }
# Paths come from HEAD, so unstaging a file (git rm --cached) doesn't hide it.
changed=$(git diff --name-only HEAD -- '*CLAUDE.md' '*AGENTS.md' '.claude/rules/*') \
  || { echo "revert hook: git diff failed" >&2; exit 2; }
[ -z "$changed" ] && exit 0
printf '%s\n' "$changed" | while IFS= read -r p; do
  git restore --source=HEAD --staged --worktree -- ":(literal)$p" || exit 1
done || { echo "REVERT FAILED for: $changed" >&2; exit 2; }
echo "Reverted uncommitted change to: $changed" >&2
exit 2
```

Save the script as `.claude/revert.sh` in the repo root, creating `.claude/` if needed. Run `chmod +x .claude/revert.sh`. Merge the entries below as in Attempt 1 ([hooks docs](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks)).

A command that wrote the file and then exited non-zero skipped `PostToolUse` in my test; `Stop` caught the change at the end of the turn. Until then the changed file remained on disk. The docs also list `PostToolUseFailure` for failed tool executions. I have not tested it with this script ([hooks docs](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks)).

```json
{
  "hooks": {
    "PostToolUse": [
      { "matcher": "*",
        "hooks": [{ "type": "command", "command": "\"$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR\"/.claude/revert.sh" }] }
    ],
    "Stop": [
      { "hooks": [{ "type": "command", "command": "\"$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR\"/.claude/revert.sh" }] }
    ]
  }
}
```

The results:

| Write | Result |
|---|---|
| obfuscated append through Bash | reverted |
| append, then `git add` | reverted |
| append to a nested `sub/CLAUDE.md`, then `git add` | reverted |
| `git rm --cached CLAUDE.md`, then append | reverted |
| `git rm CLAUDE.md` | restored |
| append in a command that exits 1 | reverted at the end of the turn by `Stop` |
| append, `git add`, then `git commit` | **kept**: the commit becomes the new baseline |

The hook skips untracked files, `CLAUDE.local.md`, settings, hook scripts and paths outside the repo, including auto memory. It also mishandles file names containing tabs or newlines, which git prints in quoted form. Restoring a file cannot undo a command that already ran or remove text the agent already loaded into context.

My first version also had a bug worth sharing. It ran `git checkout -- CLAUDE.md AGENTS.md 2>/dev/null`. The repo had no AGENTS.md, so git rejected the whole command, the error went to `/dev/null`, and the hook still reported success. The model said the change was reverted. The file still had the line. Test your hooks against the attack, and make them fail loudly.

## Against code already running as you

Use isolation to limit direct writes. Use remote review to keep poisoned changes out of shared branches. These controls protect different stages of the attack.

- **Run unattended agents in a container or as a separate OS user,** with read-only mounts or file permissions controlled by another account for the repo's instructions and settings. Keep your real home directory unmounted; expose only the files and credentials the task needs. If you use file permissions, protect parent directories too so the agent cannot replace protected files through a writable parent.
- **Install dependencies inside that boundary too.** Running an install in your main shell gives its scripts your main account's access, as the [Cisco case](https://blogs.cisco.com/ai/identifying-and-remediating-a-persistent-memory-compromise-in-claude-code) demonstrates.
- **Enforce review on the remote.** CODEOWNERS on its own only requests reviewers. Turn on branch protection or a ruleset with "require review from code owners" for CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, `.cursor/rules/` and `.claude/`, and protect CODEOWNERS itself ([GitHub docs](https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/managing-your-repositorys-settings-and-features/customizing-your-repository/about-code-owners)). Require those checks for the actors pushing changes. Review gates merges into the protected branch; your local working tree remains a separate exposure.
- **Update.** The historical fixes were [v2.1.50 for Cisco's system-prompt path](https://blogs.cisco.com/ai/identifying-and-remediating-a-persistent-memory-compromise-in-claude-code) and [v2.1.2 for CVE-2026-25725](https://osv.dev/vulnerability/CVE-2026-25725). Those fixes do not make writable memory safe.

## Make memory reviewable

These checks help detect poisoned text from either threat. They do not prevent direct writes.

- **Put auto memory under git.** Run `git init` in `~/.claude/projects/<project>/memory/` and commit after sessions you trust. `git diff HEAD` shows changes to tracked files; check `git status --short` for new files too. Review the diff before accepting a new baseline. This history is an audit aid; code with the same access can change it too.
- **Turn auto memory off where you don't need it.** This removes that loading route; instruction files and hooks remain separate. Set `autoMemoryEnabled: false` in settings or `CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_AUTO_MEMORY=1` in the environment ([Claude Code docs](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory)). That is the [default-off stance from the memory post](https://blog.carnav.in/how-ai-agents-remember/), applied to your own tools.
- **Scan instruction files for hidden Unicode** before loading an unfamiliar repo, and in a pre-commit hook for later changes ([Pillar Security](https://pillar.security/blog/new-vulnerability-in-github-copilot-and-cursor-how-hackers-can-weaponize-code-agents)).

This scan rejects zero-width characters, bidirectional overrides and isolates, and Unicode tag characters, the ones used to smuggle invisible ASCII. It exits 2 if an argument isn't a regular file it can open. It uses Perl because macOS `grep` has no `-P`. It doesn't catch look-alike letters or text that only an editor hides, so a pass only means it found none of the listed code points in the files supplied.

```sh
#!/bin/sh
# scan-hidden-unicode.sh FILE...: fail on invisible or direction-changing characters
perl -CSD -e '
  my $bad = 0;
  for my $f (@ARGV) {
    (-f $f && open(my $h, "<", $f)) or do { print STDERR "cannot read $f\n"; exit 2 };
    while (<$h>) {
      if (/[\x{200B}-\x{200F}\x{202A}-\x{202E}\x{2060}-\x{2069}\x{FEFF}\x{E0000}-\x{E007F}]/) {
        print "$f:$.: hidden Unicode\n"; $bad = 1 }
    }
    close $h;
  }
  exit $bad' "$@"
```

**For people building their own memory handler:** validate paths against traversal ([Anthropic memory tool docs](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/tool-use/memory-tool)).

These hooks target Claude Code. Check your agent's controls before adapting them.

## Check your own setup

1. Review every file in each configured memory directory, including topic files linked from `MEMORY.md`. Claude reads those files on demand ([memory docs](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory#how-it-works)). Investigate anything you didn't expect.
2. Compare hooks in `~/.claude/settings.json` and each repo's `.claude/settings.json`, plus the scripts they invoke, against a trusted baseline.
3. Inspect `.vscode/settings.json` for approval changes, including the historical `chat.tools.autoApprove` setting above.
4. Check which instruction, memory and settings files an unattended agent can write, including agents left in [a tmux pane](https://blog.carnav.in/how-terminal-multiplexers-work/).

Read the files your agent will load before giving it access you care about. Repeat that review when those files or their baseline change.

